A||||8||mikemrq||2.8 to 3.4||||1||
Z||000000||mikemrq||12-28-2001||01:17 PM||||where can i get all the info i need to replace my 2.8 with a 3.4<BR>anywhere i can download it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Kasdan||12-28-2001||02:09 PM||||Heres all you need to know....<BR>2 easy steps.<P>1.  Remove 2.8 Block<BR>2.  Install 3.4 Block<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECOsomething||12-28-2001||02:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kasdan:<BR><B>2 easy steps.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>He's right.  Everything that bolts to the 2.8 block will bolt directly to the 3.4 block.  Basically, the only thing you may need to change out are the injectors (some here will swear that the 2.8 injectors don't flow enough).||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||12-28-2001||02:42 PM||||<BR><LI> drill for starter<BR><LI> have a neutral balanced flexplate or flywheel, depending on aplication<BR><LI> drill out threads on lowest-most left side on the rear of block, as the transmission attaching bolt will pass through this particular one opposite of the other 5<BR><LI>if engine will be used for an automatic, an area as the same above, will have to be removed from the block so as the 125-C will mate to the block.<BR><LI> you will have to plug both the cam position sensor and knock sensor locations<BR><LI> the flow rate of 2.8 to 3.4 injectors is not that critical to get the engine running, but yes, I <B>DO</B> recommend using 3.4 injectors<BR><LI> there are no computer issues in this swap - plug and play<P>I believe this is the entire list of neccessary steps to install a 3.4<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif">  <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||12-28-2001||03:28 PM||||West Coast, <BR>Question did the swap this summer. Have had problems ever since. First it wouldn't hold an even idle. Now it has blown the IAC and my mechanic is saying the prom is bad. <BR>Is there any other useful tips that can help solve these problems?||||reg||1
Z||000005||West Coast Fiero||12-28-2001||03:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Uaana:<BR><B>West Coast, <BR>Question did the swap this summer. Have had problems ever since. First it wouldn't hold an even idle. Now it has blown the IAC and my mechanic is saying the prom is bad. <BR>Is there any other useful tips that can help solve these problems?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I made a tech summary of this a while back, the problem <B>WILL</B> be ( only if the throttle adjustment screw has been messed with ) the throttle blade position. There was a reason why this adjuster was plugged from the factory, if the screw is adjusted past where it should be then the IAC will battle with the computer ( i.e. injector pulse widths ) to keep an idle. The screw should be adjusted so that the throttle blade is fully closed, but not sticking, in the throttle bore.<P> Your IAC was constantly changing its position ( measured in "counts" ) to compensate for the idle. because it was always moving through its counts it had prematurely worn out the IAC.<P> on a related note, when the above is set up correctly, a vacum leak can be detected by placing your finger over the IAC port in the bore of the throttle body while the engine is running. If the engine stalls - no leak, if it continues to run, but in an extremely low RPM - there is a leak.<P> You see, as the engine idles, the only passage that allows air in for the engine to run is the IAC port. If the throtle blade screw is mis adjusted, it will divert air past the IAC and directly into the engine, thereby throwing off the idle<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||12-29-2001||01:38 AM||||The only things I can add to Eric's list are these:<P>Since you have an '86 you should weld a short flat piece of steel to the bottom of the front motor mount bracket (to lengthen it to fit the front (right side, under the water pump) motor mount and you should also weld a stud (use a bolt from the back) to the lower motor mount (the one that looks like a little shock absorber) to accept the lower a/c bracket.<P>Also, if you use the 3.4 injectors you may want to use an adjustable fuel pressure regulator to increase the fuel pressure to the fuel rail for the larger injectors.<P>Drilling for the starter means that the starter has to be re-positioned to the opposite side of the block from where it was originally positioned on the 3.4 block. There are drill guides available for drilling the holes in the proper position.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JayLT1||12-29-2001||01:59 AM||||please correct me if im wrong, but can you just change the crank??? and use the 2.8 block??<BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86 Black GT (95LT1 on the way)||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||12-29-2001||02:52 AM||||Well Jay, you can but a 2.8 is a 2.8 ... In other words, what have you really accomplished? There are a few other differences in the 3.4 (internally) such as bore size, pistons, etc. - just changing  the crankshaft on a 2.8 will not give you a 3.4.||||reg||1
